@database "llm_openrouter"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_openrouter.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "OpenRouter SDK provider"
@{h1}OpenRouter SDK provider

This plugin sends Responses API requests through OpenRouter's official
Python SDK instead of plain httpx. It exists to be @{b}compared@{ub} with
@{tt}openai_responses@{utt} from @{" OpenAI-compatible LLM providers " link llm_openai_compat/main}:
its client is that client with one method replaced -- the one that sends the
request. Payload building, prompt caching, the OpenRouter pin, the parsing
of the answer, the retries and the hook reports are the sibling's, unchanged,
so a comparison measures the transport and nothing else.

It is an LLM provider plugin: no tools, no hooks, no panel. A model entry
uses it by naming the provider:

@{table}
Provider name | Kind | Endpoint
openrouter_sdk | chat | POST <base_url>/responses, through the SDK
@{body}

How models, profiles and fallback chains work in general is in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}.

@{code}
For users (the operator writing llm.yaml)
  @{"  How it differs from openai_responses  " link differs}
  @{"  Model entry keys  " link keys}
  @{"  What the SDK carries, and what is refused  " link sdk}
  @{"  Errors  " link errors}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node differs "How it differs from openai_responses"
@{h1}How it differs from openai_responses

Everything not listed here behaves as described for openai_responses:
@{" API keys and endpoints " link llm_openai_compat/apikeys} (the key
follows the endpoint; the default endpoint is OpenRouter),
@{" Retries and self-healing " link llm_openai_compat/retries},
@{" Reasoning " link llm_openai_compat/reasoning},
@{" Prompt caching " link llm_openai_compat/caching},
@{" OpenRouter routing " link llm_openai_compat/openrouter} and
@{" Usage, cost and the hooks " link llm_openai_compat/usage}.

@{table}
Difference | openai_responses | openrouter_sdk
transport | httpx POST | the SDK's @{tt}responses.send_async@{utt} on the same httpx client
streaming | when capabilities.streaming allows it | never: the answer arrives in one response, whatever the capabilities say
stream_silence_timeout | honoured | not wired, logged as ignored when the client is built
safety_settings | sent | refused when the client is built
fields the SDK does not know | sent | see @{" What the SDK carries " link sdk}
after a 429 on the flex tier | the retry omits service_tier | the retry sends @{tt}service_tier: "auto"@{utt} (the SDK's default)
body fields the SDK adds | -- | @{tt}store: false@{utt}, @{tt}stream: false@{utt}, @{tt}service_tier: "auto"@{utt} when none is set
extra headers | -- | the SDK's user agent; HTTP-Referer and X-OpenRouter-Title when the environment sets OPENROUTER_HTTP_REFERER or OPENROUTER_X_OPEN_ROUTER_TITLE (the SDK reads them itself)
@{body}

The SDK's own retries are switched off: every attempt is one request the
inherited loop sees, retries and reports. The answer is read from the raw
HTTP body, not from the SDK's typed result: that model makes @{tt}usage@{utt}
vanish without an error when @{tt}cost_details@{utt} lacks the two
upstream cost fields it requires, and refuses a body missing one of a dozen
fields it requires. So tokens, @{tt}cost@{utt}, @{tt}cost_details@{utt},
@{tt}is_byok@{utt} and the served-by backend arrive exactly as on
openai_responses. Reasoning tokens are passed on as the provider counts
them; they may exceed the completion tokens.

Because nothing streams, the chat shows no text or thinking while the
model works, and the read timeout is the longest wait for the whole answer
(see @{" Timeouts and streaming " link llm_openai_compat/timeouts}).
@endnode

@node keys "Model entry keys"
@{h1}Model entry keys

The factory hands the entry to the client exactly as the openai_responses
factory does; the keys mean what
@{" Model entry keys " link llm_openai_compat/keys} says for the Responses
route. The differences:

@{table}
Key | What happens here
provider | openrouter_sdk
safety_settings | a non-empty value is refused: building the client fails with "provider 'openrouter_sdk' cannot send safety_settings". An empty one builds
prompt_cache_marker_style | all three styles work; @{tt}anthropic@{utt} travels as the one top-level @{tt}cache_control@{utt}, as on openai_responses
thinking_budget | ignored, with a warning -- use thinking_level
assistant_reasoning_field, thinking_request_shape, stream_silence_timeout | logged as "not wired ... will be ignored"
provider_routing, plugins | checked through the SDK's types when the client is built; a value the SDK would drop or refuse (a numeric max_price, an unknown key or plugin id) fails the build (see @{" What the SDK carries " link sdk})
context_window | 200000 when unset or 0
request_timeout, httpx_timeouts | the Responses defaults: read 600 s unless the entry sets request_timeout, write 30 s, connect 10 s, pool 5 s
@{body}

Switching a model over is one word:

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    or-gpt-sdk:
      provider: openrouter_sdk
      model: openai/gpt-5.4
      api_key: ${OPENROUTER_API_KEY}
      thinking_level: medium
      prompt_cache_key: auto
@{body}
@endnode

@node sdk "What the SDK carries, and what is refused"
@{h1}What the SDK carries, and what is refused

The SDK takes the request as typed parameters. Every field the payload
builder produces is handed over under its own name: input, instructions,
model, tools, tool_choice, parallel_tool_calls, reasoning, temperature,
max_output_tokens, service_tier, provider, session_id, prompt_cache_key,
prompt_cache_options, cache_control, plugins, safety_identifier, text
(structured output) and the rest of the SDK's signature. Stored reasoning
items, function calls, audio and the @{tt}prompt_cache_breakpoint@{utt}
markers on content parts travel unchanged. An image without a
@{tt}detail@{utt} is sent with @{tt}detail: "auto"@{utt}, the API's own
default: the SDK requires the field.

@{h2}A field the SDK has no parameter for

It is left out of the request and logged once per field and process:
@{tt}openrouter_sdk: payload field '<name>' has no SDK parameter and is NOT
being sent@{utt}. @{tt}store@{utt} is the exception -- the SDK sends
@{tt}store: false@{utt} itself.

@{h2}A value the SDK cannot type

A typed parameter that fails the SDK's validation is either dropped whole,
without an error, or refused before anything is sent. A
@{tt}provider_routing@{utt} with a numeric @{tt}max_price@{utt} (the SDK
types prices as strings) would lose the entire routing object -- the pin,
@{tt}only@{utt}, @{tt}data_collection@{utt} -- and a routing key the SDK
does not know would be left out. Three checks keep that from happening
silently:

@{bullet}@{b}When the client is built@{ub}, provider_routing and plugins go
through the SDK's types. A value it would drop or refuse fails the build:
@{tt}provider 'openrouter_sdk' cannot send 'provider_routing' as declared
...@{utt}. Write prices as strings (@{tt}max_price: {prompt: "1"}@{utt}) or
use @{tt}provider: openai_responses@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Before each request leaves@{ub}, the body the SDK is about to
send is compared with what it was given; a missing or changed value refuses
the request: @{tt}openrouter_sdk: the SDK would not send '<field>' as given
...@{utt}. A @{tt}null@{utt} of ours that the SDK leaves out counts as
carried.
@{bullet}@{b}A request the SDK refuses to type@{ub} -- for example a
replayed tool call without an id -- is refused the same way:
@{tt}openrouter_sdk: the SDK refuses the request as built ..., nothing was
sent: <start of the refused value>@{utt}.

In the last two cases nothing goes over the wire, the refusal is reported
to the post_llm_response hooks once, and the error reaches the agent as a
@{tt}ValueError@{utt} (see @{" Errors " link errors}).
@endnode

@node errors "Errors"
@{h1}Errors

What the loop does with a response is the sibling's
(@{" Errors " link llm_openai_compat/errors}): LLMRateLimitError,
LLMQuotaExhaustedError and LLMServerError after the retries, an
httpx.HTTPStatusError for any other 4xx, the httpx timeout or transport
error once its retries are spent. What the SDK adds:

@{table}
The SDK | What the client does
raises one of its errors with a response attached (an error status, or a 200 its typed model refuses) | hands that response to the loop, which judges it by status and body like any other
raises one of its errors without a response | reported as @{tt}httpx.TransportError@{utt}, so it is retried like a dropped connection
raises an httpx error | passed on unchanged: timeouts and network errors are retried
would drop or change a field, or refuses to type the request (see @{" What the SDK carries " link sdk}) | ValueError before sending, not retried
@{body}

The agent server's fallback chain takes over on the typed LLM errors and
on httpx errors (@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}). The
ValueError of a refused field is not one of them: the run ends with its
message, since every request of that entry would be refused the same way.

At build time: ValueError for a missing key or an unknown dialect value (as
on openai_responses), for safety_settings, and for provider_routing or
plugins the SDK cannot carry.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

Exactly what openai_responses sends -- messages, media, tools and
structured output are built by the same code; see
@{" What the model sees " link llm_openai_compat/model}. Only the bytes
around them differ as listed in
@{" How it differs " link differs}. No thinking or text is streamed to
the chat while the model works.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable: the core finds the plugin by its
@{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} (@{tt}type = ["llm-provider"]@{utt},
@{tt}provides = ["openrouter_sdk"]@{utt}, default base_url
https://openrouter.ai/api/v1) and loads @{tt}provider.py@{utt} when a model
entry names the provider. No @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} entry, no
allowlist line.

@{h2}Dependencies

@{tt}openrouter>=1.1.108@{utt}, declared in @{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} and
folded into @{tt}requirements/all.txt@{utt}; it is imported when a request
is sent, or already at build for an entry that sets
@{tt}provider_routing@{utt} or @{tt}plugins@{utt}. The SDK pins @{tt}pydantic<2.13@{utt}, which caps the whole
application. The plugin also needs llm_openai_compat, whose client and
timeout defaults it uses.

@{h2}Where things live

Nothing on disk. Changes to @{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} reach an agent when it
builds its client.
@endnode
